.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt AZRAM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm azram
.Nd compressed cache in front of swap
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "options AZRAM"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
keeps anonymous pages that are about to be written to swap in memory
instead, compressed with LZ4.
A page faulted back in from
.Nm
is decompressed in microseconds, where reading it from a hard disk takes
milliseconds.
.Pp
.Nm
sits in front of a swap device and does not replace one: each page it keeps
has its slot on a swap device, allocated as usual, and
.Nm
can always decline a page and let it be written to that slot.
It does so for a page that does not compress well, when its pool is full,
and when memory is short, so that the system pages no worse than without
.Nm .
At least one swap device must be configured; see
.Xr swapctl 8 .
.Ss Pageout
When the pagedaemon writes a cluster of pages to swap,
.Nm
looks at each page first.
A page whose bytes all have the same value, such as a page of zeros, is
kept as that value alone.
Any other page is compressed; it is kept if it shrinks to
.Va vm.azram.reject_size
bytes or less, and if its pool can take it without growing beyond
.Va vm.azram.pool_max .
The pages kept are done at once; the others are written to the swap
device, the consecutive ones in one write each.
.Pp
The compressed data goes to a pool with a size class per 1/64 page, up to
7/8 page.
Each class takes memory in chunks of 2 to 16 pages, so that little is
lost to rounding;
.Va vm.azram.pool_max
counts the memory of these chunks, however full they are.
.Pp
.Nm
never waits for memory, and leaves the kernel's reserve of free pages
alone: when free memory is that low, a page that would need more memory
for the pool goes to the swap device, while one that fits in the memory
the pool has already is still kept.
The buffers for compression are allocated at boot.
.Ss Pagein
A page fault on a page that went to swap asks
.Nm
before it reads the swap device.
The page stays in
.Nm
afterwards, as long as the system keeps its swap slot: if the page is
paged out again unchanged, it need not be compressed again.
When a process exits or frees the memory, or the page changes, the slot is
freed and so is the compressed copy.
.Pp
A compressed page that does not decompress to exactly one page means that
memory is corrupt; the system panics rather than give a process wrong
data.
.Ss Swap devices
Removing a swap device with
.Xr swapctl 8
pages in everything it holds, from
.Nm
or from the device, as without
.Nm .
.Pp
The swap usage that
.Xr swapctl 8
and
.Xr vmstat 1
report includes the pages
.Nm
holds, since they keep their slots.
.Xr azramctl 8
shows how much
.Nm
holds and how much went to the device.
.Ss Measuring
With
.Va vm.azram.measure
set and
.Va vm.azram.enable
clear, every page on its way to swap is compressed only to count its size,
and then written to the swap device as without
.Nm .
.Xr azramctl 8
shows these sizes and what each value of
.Va vm.azram.reject_size
would keep.
The counters of reads from the swap device, and of the time they take,
work with
.Nm
off too.
.Ss Turning it on and off
.Va vm.azram.enable
is 0 when the system boots.
.Pa /etc/rc.d/azram
sets it if
.Va azram
is set in
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
as it is by default.
Clearing it stops
.Nm
from taking new pages; the pages it holds can still be paged in.
.Pp
On x86, booting with the
.Fl 4
flag of
.Xr boot 8 ,
which the default boot menu offers as
.Dq Boot without prefetch or azram ,
keeps
.Pa /etc/rc.d/azram
from turning
.Nm
on for that boot.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width vm.azram.boot_disabled
.It Va vm.azram.enable
Keep pages in
.Nm ;
0 at boot.
.It Va vm.azram.measure
Compress the pages that go to swap only to count their sizes, while
.Va vm.azram.enable
is clear; 0 by default.
.It Va vm.azram.pool_max
Bytes of memory the compressed pages may take, at most the size of
physical memory; a quarter of it by default.
Lowering it below what the pool holds now only stops the pool from
growing.
.It Va vm.azram.reject_size
Largest size, in bytes, that a compressed page may have to be kept, from
1/64 to 7/8 page; 3/4 page (3072 bytes with 4 KB pages) by default.
.It Va vm.azram.boot_disabled
Read-only: 1 if the boot flag kept
.Nm
off for this boot.
.It Va vm.azram.stats
.Vt struct azram_stats ,
as defined in
.In sys/azram.h :
what
.Nm
holds now (pages kept compressed and of one value, the size of their data,
the memory of the pool and of the metadata), and counters of the pages
offered, kept and declined for each reason, paged in, and freed, the time
spent compressing and decompressing, the pages written to and read from
the swap device and the time spent waiting for those reads, and the
compressed sizes of the pages looked at.
Writing it, which only root may do, resets the counters.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr vmstat 1 ,
.Xr rc.conf 5 ,
.Xr azramctl 8 ,
.Xr boot 8 ,
.Xr swapctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
was written for AutumnBSD.
.Sh CAVEATS
When the pool reaches
.Va vm.azram.pool_max ,
new pages go to the swap device while older ones stay in memory, even if
they will not be needed for a long time.
.Nm
does not write its pages out to the swap device yet.
.Pp
Swap encryption
.Pq Va vm.swap_encrypt
applies to what is written to the swap device only; the pages
.Nm
holds are in memory, like any other memory of a process, and are part of a
crash dump.
.Pp
How long a page fault takes depends on whether the page was in
.Nm ,
and so on how well its contents compress.
.Sh BUGS
A compressed page that is freed leaves a hole in its chunk; the pool does
not move pages to fill such holes, so a fragmented pool may hold more
memory than its data needs.
.Xr azramctl 8
shows how much.
